Just a thought on the wormhole issue - I may be mixing up wormholes
with the kind of matter that leads to inflation here, but I've read
that inflation (which I don't understand) started when the universe
in some sense super-cooled during the early parts of the Big Bang
expansion.  The "phase change" ("freezing?") from this unnatural super-
cooled state into the regular vacuum powered inflation.

This suggests to me that other phase changes may occur as the universe
continues to cool.  If the universe expands forever, it will cool to
arbitrarily low temperatures, and there may be some threshold, very close
to absolute zero (<10^-50K?) at which more "spontaneous symmetry breaking"
will occur, new particles appear, and a new kind of vacuum come into
existance.  Now, piling speculation on speculation, if we could super-cool
some region of space then perhaps it would develop into a state where it
had negative energy in the same sense that the supercooled universe did in
the moments immediately preceding inflation.

