I think what happens is that the definition of "local" changes.  The
space on the other side of the wormhole or time gateway becomes local,
so all of the physical laws are still locally conserved.  As long as
the portal itself doesn't just "pop" into existance but in some sense
pulls apart from some initially shared space (I don't know if they are
actually created in this way) and then moves smoothly from then on,
there shouldn't be any discontinuities.

Deutsch's article on time travel portals which we discussed here some
time back viewed them as connecting different parallel universes in
order to eliminate the paradoxes.  One of the side effects of his point
of view was that you could move mass from one universe to another,
in effect bringing more mass from other universes into this one, if
desired.  That way we need never run out, I guess.

