Date: Sat, 10 Apr 93 13:17:58 PDT
From: hal@alumni.cco.caltech.edu (Hal Finney)
Subject: FTL: traversable wormholes

The idea of wormholes distant in space and time repelling each other
seems hard to credit, and even the idea of them overloading each other
and exploding seems strange.

But I remember an article in Scientific American a few years ago about
these crystals that would reflect light in whatever direction it came
in, but they would reflect it at higher intensity than it arrived.
(They had their own power source to provide the energy.)

The result was that if you brought a shiny object up near the crystal,
a light beam would spontaneously spring into being between the crystal
and the object.  They had a picture on the cover of a soup ladle being
held near the crystal, with a beam of light joining them.  The idea
was that any random photons which headed into the crystal from the direction
of the spoon would get reflected back to the spoon, where they would
reflect back to the crystal, and so on.  As long as the boost from the
crystal was greater than the decrease from the imperfect reflection from
the spoon, there would be positive feedback and a beam would appear.

The authors implied that this was actually rather hazardous, as even
a shiny belt buckle could cause the feedback to start up and a light
beam to form.  If the crystal were pumped powerfully enough this could
presumably be dangerous.

It seems like something similar would happen with the wormholes as they
got into causal loops.  Any photon from the ancient end would travel
to the new end, and go back in time to the ancient end.  Now two photons
can travel.  They go back, and now there are four.  This whole buildup
would happen outside of spacetime, hence they would presumably appear
instantly.  So if the wormhole started out non-causally connected, as
it approached causal connection single photons could turn into repeated
streams of photons, which would get closer and closer together as causal
connection was approached.  An infinite flux would appear just as it
became paradoxical.

Hal
