> phonetic script. Same goes for the related Chinese writing system.
> If "Look-Say" were _that_ much inferior, these places would have
> lower literacy rates than they apparently do.

But the problems facing someone learning to read English are very
different from those facing a Japanese or Chinese learner.  True, since
the Chinese script is not phonetic, the learner has to learn and
arbitrary pronunciation associated with each one.  But, each such
pronunciation is a single syllable, and there are only about 400
distinct syllables in the language.  I.e. for each character the
learner has to learn which of the 400 possible pronunciations, all
known in advance, goes with it.  The number of possible syllables in
English is larger by an order of magnitude, and the number of actual words
(which is the relevant measure) that a student has to learn is 
~10,000-15,000, not all of which can be known in advance.  (Even so,
Chinese elementary school children are taught a phonetic script, and
when new characters are introduced the pronunciation is always
indicated.  Beginning readers have all the characters glossed with
little phonetic symbols so the kids can remember which word they
represent).
     The Japanese case is a little more complicated, but one relevant
fact is that Japanese children are only required to learn about
1,000 Kanji (that's total, all the way through high school).  Everything
else is written phonetically with kana.  So the Japanese child's
task is very similar to that of a child learning to read English
phonetically--learning a phonetic system which makes sense, and
memorizing a large but finite set of arbitrary representations along
with it.

