From: rclark@nyx.cs.du.edu (Robert W. F. Clark)
Subject: A New UseNET through Reputation Markets

I had been following the Reputation Markets thread with a certain
amount of apathy, not really seeing the point of such an endeavor,
but when considering the previously-mentioned thread "A New UseNET,"
and an email response I received to my mailing, I realized that 
that something on the order of a reputation market could easily
solve the problem of low signal-to-noise ratio.

The idea is somewhat analagous to a 'Best of UseNET' anthology
service, except that everyone would choose the interactive filters
which they would use to 'turn up the brightness knob.'  

This is the germ of the idea; when reading an article, instead
of replying to the sender or posting a response, one could 'o,' or
register an opinion, either by a market system (which I think many
on UseNET would find arbitrary and confusing) or simply by answering
a series of brief questions with numeric answers; essentially, by
evaluating their opinion of that person on a number of axes;
Entertaining/Boring; Conservative/Liberal; Hot/Cold (level of
interactiveness; the McCluhan-esque name could be changed to something
more clearly understandable); Useful/Useless; and a simple 1 to 10
as to how well you like that poster.  Combining this with the
arbitron data for high-volume posters, one essentially has, then,
a reputation market with a REAL application.

Perhaps a control newsgroup could be created, or a server which
accepts TCP connections from a client software package external
to trn; this newsgroup would be 'reputation data.'  It would
require a recognized PGP signature to post to this group, as
otherwise it would be possible to trash someone's reputation.
This is why it would probably not be best to distribute the popularity
data by the same means as the material the popularity of which is
to be judged.

Then, choosing acceptable ranges of axes as a mask, one reads
interactively, either deciding to be entertained or informed,
irritated or whatever.  

It is more likely that several competing markets would develop,
as different groups of people opened 'Extropian reputation markets,'
'Mystics reputation markets,' 'Druggies reputation markets,' and
so forth.

Perhaps some of the more idiotic flamers; 'idiotic' being a key word,
as I see nothing wrong with a witty flame; might shape up if they
actually could, to paraphrase Burns, see themselves as others see them.

In any case, it would allow both new UseNET readers to find out
whether the guy who just flamed them has a reputation for being
an idiot, or is 90% recommended by UseNET posters.  

The popularity contest would result in the most popular posters 
striving to become more popular, and thus posting more useful,
informative posts.  If people become annoyed with the volume of
a poster, they could register their opinion on that issue as
well; or in fact on any useful axis that market rated.

