Date: Sat, 10 Apr 93 23:13:37 GMT
From: price@price.demon.co.uk (Michael Clive Price)
Subject: FTL: traversable wormholes 

Perry asks about the status of conservation laws with time travel.
The wormhole business Robin and I have been outlining doesn't permit
time travel in the go-back-and-try-to-shoot-your-grandfather sense. 
However, even if it did, there would be no problem.  

Conservation laws are temporarily violated in the vicinity of a time
machine, but only in well behaved ways.  Eventually (ie when the time
machine has stopped operating) they are restored.  You end up with the
same quantities of all the requisite conserved quantities as you started
with.  Eg if I send back a kilo mass a hundred years from 2200 to 2100
then for that century the universe has an 'extra' kilo.  But in the long
run it all sorts itself out.

Things are not so straight forward if the wormhole travel takes you to
a different or parallel universe (as Dr David Deutsch has suggested). 
Then the matter that disappears or appears is lost or gained for ever
and the conservation laws are more drastically broken.  Presumably
conservation laws would only apply summed across all the universes. 
Although I have no problems with conservation laws being redefined
whenever necessary, I don't think a convincing case has been made for
travel to parallel worlds.

